<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>GUI recording and outcome checks · Umicom</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css">@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a><a href="../products.html">Applications</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>GUI recording and outcome checks</h1><p>A recorder keeps observations. A test adds an expectation and decides whether that expectation was met. Keeping these two ideas separate prevents a clicked button from being mistaken for a completed operation.</p><p>The recording model is toolkit-neutral C. The GTK adapter supplies a local recording panel and PNG capture. Applications that use the shared titlebar receive the entry point from Framework; another host can call the same public adapter function.</p>
<h2>Find the implementation</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File or target</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/interaction_recording.h</code></td><td>Public recording entries, lifecycle and output-sink contract.</td></tr><tr><td><code>src/ui/interaction_recording.c</code></td><td>Bounded copied entries, input validation, JSON and HTML escaping.</td></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/automation.h</code></td><td>Existing scenarios and reports, plus a check that all expected steps succeeded.</td></tr><tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/interaction_recording_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Window-scoped input observation, consent, PNG rendering and local exports.</td></tr><tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/workstation/shell_header_gtk4.c</code></td><td>The Report problem button in the existing shared titlebar.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests/gui_recording/</code></td><td>Core regression cases and the native GTK fixture.</td></tr><tr><td><code>examples/gui_recording/main.c</code></td><td>Complete Notes file-check example shown below.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Umicom::ui</code> / <code>Umicom::ui_gtk4</code></td><td>Core model and GTK adapter link targets respectively.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Follow one observation</h2><p>The adapter receives an input event, resolves an existing stable automation ID when available and copies an entry into <code>UmiUiRecording</code>. It then writes that entry to the session log. The event is still delivered to the application normally. An input observation has <code>outcomeKnown = 0</code>: the recorder did not decide whether Save, Build or another command succeeded.</p><p>The core model does not access files or databases. It sends output bytes to a callback supplied by its owner. The GTK adapter implements that callback with a local file stream. These files are user-requested exports, not another database or a replacement for the Data Server.</p>
<h2>Use the recording contract</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Operations</th><th>How to use them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>UmiUiRecordingCreate</code> / <code>Destroy</code></td><td>Create paused storage with a capacity from 1 to 4,096. Identifiers are copied; destroy releases the entries. A failed create clears the output pointer.</td></tr><tr><td><code>SetActive</code> / <code>IsActive</code></td><td>Start and pause without clearing entries. A full recording cannot resume; export it and create another session.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Append</code> / <code>At</code></td><td>Validate and copy an entry, then copy it out by zero-based index. Timestamps must not go backwards. Rejected entries do not alter earlier observations.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Count</code> / <code>LimitReached</code></td><td>Inspect retained size and the explicit full-session stop. No older entry is silently overwritten.</td></tr><tr><td><code>AppendAutomation</code></td><td>Import an existing driver-step result. Input values, observed text and free-text driver messages are omitted from the imported row.</td></tr><tr><td><code>WriteJson</code> / <code>WriteHtml</code></td><td>Stream complete escaped exports. The caller owns and closes its sink. A sink failure stops subsequent writes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>WriteEntryJson</code></td><td>Write one existing entry as a JSON line, allowing incremental logging without serialising all previous entries.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationReportRequireSuccess</code></td><td>Require a nonempty expected step count, exact completion and zero failed steps. It does not add missing assertions to a scenario.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Call these operations on the recording’s owner thread. Do not modify or destroy it inside an output callback. Fixed-size entry fields must contain terminated UTF-8. Only a simple PNG basename is accepted for an attachment; a path such as <code>../private.png</code> is rejected.</p><p>Start with a small capacity in tests to exercise its full condition. Also test invalid UTF-8, an unterminated field, decreasing timestamps and a failing output sink. Memory retained by the recorder is bounded, but the complete application has other allocations and is not limited to that amount.</p>
<h2>Connect a GTK application</h2><p>The host calls this after its window is presented, from the GTK owner thread:</p><pre><code>#include &quot;umicom/ui/gtk4/interaction_recording.h&quot;

/* Inside an existing GTK callback; window is the host&#x27;s mapped GtkWindow. */
UmiStatus status = UmiGtk4RecordingPanelShow(window, &quot;umicom-notes&quot;);
if (status != UMI_STATUS_OK) {
    /* Present or log the returned failure through the host&#x27;s error service. */
}</code></pre><p>The panel starts off and weakly observes the owner. It closes with its parent. Tests can use <code>UmiGtk4RecordingPanelShowAt</code> with an absolute temporary directory so their reports do not enter a real user profile.</p><p><code>UmiGtk4RecordingCapturePng</code> renders one mapped widget into a new PNG file. The caller must obtain consent. It rejects an existing destination, private visible subtrees, an unavailable renderer and oversized images. The capture contains GTK content only, not another process, native window decoration or the mouse cursor.</p><p>For a custom private view, call <code>UmiGtk4RecordingSetPrivate(view, 1)</code>. This blocks screenshots containing that subtree and suppresses its identifier in input observations. <code>UmiGtk4RecordingContainsPrivate</code> lets a test check the policy without taking an image. It does not detect every type of confidential business information automatically.</p>
<h2>Make the outcome check independent</h2><p>The following example creates a temporary Notes file and an automation scenario with two steps. The first requests Save. The second reads the file to check its contents. The controlled example driver is deliberately not a desktop driver: it teaches the assertion and report contracts without needing a screen.</p><p>Run the example normally, then run it with <code>--fail-save</code>. In the second run the simulated button handler returns success but writes nothing. The file assertion fails, and the executable returns a nonzero exit status. This demonstrates why “the click returned” is not enough.</p>
<pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_GUI_RECORDING_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<h2>Build and run the example</h2><p>From a configured Applications checkout with tests enabled:</p><pre><code>cmake --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-recording-example
.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-recording-example.exe
.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-recording-example.exe --fail-save
$LASTEXITCODE</code></pre><p>The normal run returns 0. The deliberate no-op run returns 1. A setup or export failure returns 2. The JSON separates driver-step outcomes from observed human input. Add <code>--html</code> and redirect output to a new file to inspect the same results in a browser.</p><p>With an installed Framework SDK, the example’s own CMake project uses <code>find_package(UmicomFramework CONFIG REQUIRED)</code>. Set <code>CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH</code> to that SDK’s installation prefix. Its installed source location is <code>share/umicom-framework/examples/gui_recording</code>.</p>
<h2>Desktop automation and repeatable scenarios</h2><p>Desktop tests can locate controls, perform actions and check results in the same general way that browser tests do. For GTK, use stable identifiers in the in-process Framework driver, then add external pointer/keyboard scenarios for focus, menus, dialogs and display scaling.</p><p>The in-process driver and an operating-system input driver test different layers. Activating a widget can help test its binding but can bypass problems with pointer hit testing or keyboard routing. Use both kinds of test for important workflows.</p><p>Dogtail uses AT-SPI to automate Linux desktop applications and documents GTK4 and GNOME Wayland requirements. FlaUI wraps Windows UI Automation; inspect the accessibility tree of the exact application build before relying on it. GTK accessibility support and bridge behaviour depend on the platform and build. A Framework automation tag is not automatically a Windows UIA AutomationId.</p><p>Appium provides a Selenium-like Windows driver, but its documentation warns that the underlying Microsoft WinAppDriver server has not been maintained since 2022. Treat it as a compatibility evaluation, not an automatic default.</p><p>Recorded human observations are not automatically replayable tests. A reusable scenario still needs a disposable starting state, deliberate inputs and explicit assertions. Do not replay financial or destructive actions against a live environment.</p>
<p><a href="gui-control-checks.html">Inspect control IDs and add state assertions</a> when a recorded action needs a repeatable test.</p><h2>Turn a recording into a useful defect</h2><p>Keep the recorded sequence and add the expected result, actual result, build identity and reproduction frequency. Label a missing stable control ID as a testability gap. Label a missing expected file update as a functional failure. Do not infer a missing callback merely because a visual change did not appear; trace the action through its owning command and service.</p><p>When a fix is ready, rerun the same scenario and retain both the failed and successful results. One shared Framework fix may repair several applications. Link those affected scenarios instead of creating unrelated copies of the implementation.</p>
<h2>Further reading</h2><p><a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/section-accessibility.html">GTK accessibility</a> · <a href="https://gitlab.com/dogtail/dogtail">Dogtail</a> · <a href="https://github.com/FlaUI/FlaUI">FlaUI</a> · <a href="https://appium.io/docs/en/latest/ecosystem/drivers/">Appium drivers</a> · <a href="../GUI_RECORDING.html">Using the recorder</a></p></main></body></html>
